LOGBOOK.md-538-### 1527 — Relay UDP registry owns bounded unbound descriptors (TASK-260715-xw5dxc)
LOGBOOK.md-539-- IMPLEMENTATION: A single Go owner goroutine now linearizes relay association admission, descriptor use, activity, close, expiry, generation replacement, and cancellation. Identity is the session generation plus nonzero client association ID plus a monotonically increasing local incarnation, so stale timer/activity/close work cannot affect a reused numeric ID.
LOGBOOK.md-540-- RESOURCE POLICY: Association, family-specific socket, logical timer, and pending-close credits are checked before descriptor creation and report finite aggregate counters. One indexed deadline heap and one physical monotonic timer bound timer/task state; expiry events consume reserved pending-close credit until observed. IPv4 and IPv6 descriptors are created lazily and closed exactly once across normal, partial-failure, expiry, session, process, and cancellation paths.
LOGBOOK.md-541-- SOCKET POLICY: Production descriptors are standard-library UDP sockets configured nonblocking and close-on-exec. They are unconnected and deliberately unbound at registry creation; no public listener or privileged port is created. A later bounded `sendto` owner may allow the kernel to assign an ephemeral source port without changing registry ownership.

